<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Actium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/31-bce">31 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/final-war-of-the-roman-republic">Final War of the Roman Republic</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Actium was a naval battle fought in 31 BCE off western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> between Octavian and the combined forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a>. Octavian's fleet under Agrippa won decisively. The victory led to the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>, and it made Octavian, the future Augustus, sole ruler of the Roman world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium.jpg" alt="Battle of Actium" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Landon · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinaceli_Actium_reliefs_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> of Octavian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Lepidus was renewed in 37 BCE but broke down. Antony left his wife Octavia Minor, Octavian's sister, and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a>. In 34 BCE he and Cleopatra raised her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a>, said to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s, to power as King of Kings, which was seen as a threat to Roman republican tradition. Octavian, whom Caesar had adopted, began a propaganda war, claiming that Antony meant to set up a monarchy and move the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><p>In 32 BCE both consuls and a third of the Senate sided with Antony and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Antony divorced Octavia and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> with Cleopatra, where a vast fleet gathered. The publication of Antony's will provoked an outburst of feeling in Rome, and Antony was deprived of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>. The Senate declared war on Cleopatra, which was understood to mean Antony, and left him without legal authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Antony's fleet lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a> and his army camped near Actium. In 31 BCE Octavian's general Marcus Agrippa raided the Greek coasts and cut Antony's communications with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Disease and desertion afflicted Antony's camp, and malaria left many ships short of rowers. One of his generals, Quintus Dellius, defected with his battle plans. His heavy ships were hard to board but less manoeuvrable than Octavian's smaller Liburnian vessels.</p><p>On 2 September the fleets met outside the gulf. Octavian refused to be drawn out, and the battle raged through the afternoon without result. Cleopatra's squadron then retreated without fighting, and Antony, believing all was lost, followed her in a smaller vessel. The ships left behind fought on until after nightfall, and were captured or sunk. J. M. Carter gives a different account, in which Antony deliberately broke through a gap in Octavian's line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some 19 legions and 12,000 cavalry left Antony in the night, and his camp was occupied the next day. Antony was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 30 BCE and stabbed himself on false news that Cleopatra was dead; she killed herself on 12 August. Octavian had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a> killed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> became a Roman province, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>. He took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">Princeps</a>, and in 27 BCE the Senate gave him the name Augustus. Historians generally regard this as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. He founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-epirus">Nicopolis</a>, meaning City of Victory, opposite Actium.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Actium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their defeat by Octavian at the <b>Battle of Actium</b> in 31 BCE, Rome annexed Egypt in 30 BCE, ending the Ptolemaic state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of Antony and Cleopatra VII at the <b>Battle of Actium</b> in 31 BCE, Octavian marched on Alexandria, which fell on 1 August 30 BCE; Antony and Cleopatra took their own lives, and Cleopatra's son by Julius Caesar, Ptolemy XV Caesarion, was put to death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon in 49 BCE, his assassination in 44 BCE and the wars of the Second Triumvirate ended in Octavian's decisive victory over Mark Antony and Cleopatra VII at the <b>Battle of Actium</b> in 31 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Octavian defeated Antony and Cleopatra VII at <b>Actium</b> in 31 BCE and annexed Egypt in 30 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Battle of Actium</b> in 31 BCE, Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa began a building programme on his own property in the Campus Martius, creating in 29–19 BCE a complex of three buildings aligned from south to north: the Baths of Agrippa, the Basilica of Neptune and the Pantheon.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The league faded under Roman rule; after <b>Actium</b> in 31 BCE Augustus moved much of its population to his new city of Nicopolis.</blockquote><a href="/state/acarnanian-league">Acarnanian League</a></li></ul></section>
